  "poland-convergence": {
    "quick": "Poland’s convergence story is about how a post-communist economy narrowed the income gap with Western Europe.\n\nAfter 1989, Poland combined market reforms, EU integration, foreign investment and a large domestic market. The topic matters because it changes real economic choices: where firms invest, how governments spend, which workers gain opportunities, how households protect their income, and how countries position themselves in global competition.\n\nThe key mechanisms are concrete: Manufacturing, infrastructure funds, education and supply-chain integration helped raise productivity.. Examples such as EU cohesion funds, German supply chains, Warsaw services, automotive and appliances exports show that this is not an abstract trend. The challenges are demographics, rule-of-law credibility, energy transition and moving beyond lower-cost manufacturing. Poland’s next phase depends on innovation, defense investment and whether it can keep converging as wages rise.",
    "medium": "01 — The basic story\nPoland’s convergence story is about how a post-communist economy narrowed the income gap with Western Europe. After 1989, Poland combined market reforms, EU integration, foreign investment and a large domestic market. That starting point explains why the issue cannot be reduced to one statistic or one headline. It touches production, finance, infrastructure, trust and political choices at the same time.\n\n02 — How the mechanism works\nThe process works through specific channels: Manufacturing, infrastructure funds, education and supply-chain integration helped raise productivity.. These channels decide who receives investment, who bears costs and who gains bargaining power. In practice, the topic moves through companies, households, government budgets, supply chains and financial markets.\n\n03 — The examples that make it visible\nThe clearest examples are EU cohesion funds, German supply chains, Warsaw services, automotive and appliances exports. They show the physical and institutional side of the issue: ports, factories, banks, currencies, power grids, farms, mines, data centers or strategic corridors. Once those systems are stressed, the effects spread beyond the original place.\n\n04 — The main tension\nThe challenges are demographics, rule-of-law credibility, energy transition and moving beyond lower-cost manufacturing. This is why the issue is not simply a story of progress or failure. It usually creates benefits and vulnerabilities at the same time. New investment can raise productivity, but it can also produce dependence. Strategic assets can create leverage, but also make a country exposed to external shocks.\n\n05 — What comes next\nPoland’s next phase depends on innovation, defense investment and whether it can keep converging as wages rise. The useful question is whether the current model builds lasting capacity: better skills, deeper firms, stronger infrastructure, reliable institutions and more resilience. If it does, the story becomes a long-term transformation. If it does not, the headline can fade while the underlying weakness remains.",
    "deep": "01 — Why this topic matters\nPoland’s convergence story is about how a post-communist economy narrowed the income gap with Western Europe. After 1989, Poland combined market reforms, EU integration, foreign investment and a large domestic market. The importance of the topic comes from the fact that it connects everyday economic life with larger systems of power. Prices, jobs, trade, public budgets, energy security and national strategy can all be affected by the same underlying mechanism.\n\n02 — The historical background\nThe current situation did not appear overnight. It grew out of earlier choices about infrastructure, trade, finance, technology, state capacity and international positioning. Some countries built advantages over decades; others entered the story because a global shock suddenly made their geography, resources or institutions more valuable.\n\n03 — The operating mechanism\nThe central mechanism is built around Manufacturing, infrastructure funds, education and supply-chain integration helped raise productivity.. This mechanism matters because it turns a general trend into specific incentives. Firms decide whether to invest or leave. Governments decide whether to subsidize, regulate, borrow or liberalize. Households decide whether to save, migrate, consume, protest or adapt.\n\n04 — The actors involved\nThe main actors are not only governments. They also include multinational firms, banks, exporters, importers, workers, consumers, regulators, local communities and foreign powers. Their interests do not always align. One group may want lower prices, another wants security, another wants jobs, and another wants strategic control.\n\n05 — Concrete examples\nThe issue becomes clearer through examples such as EU cohesion funds, German supply chains, Warsaw services, automotive and appliances exports. These examples matter because they show where the abstract forces touch the ground. A shipping route, a currency market, a dam, a mine, a semiconductor plant or a payment system can reveal more about the world economy than a broad growth number.\n\n06 — The development question\nThe development question is whether the benefits spread into the wider economy. A country can attract investment without building local suppliers. It can export resources without creating good jobs. It can become strategically important without becoming more equal or productive. The strongest outcomes appear when external demand is converted into domestic capability.\n\n07 — The vulnerability\nThe challenges are demographics, rule-of-law credibility, energy transition and moving beyond lower-cost manufacturing. This vulnerability is important because modern economies are interconnected. A local problem can become an international shock when it affects food, energy, transport, finance, technology or security. The more concentrated the system is, the more damaging a disruption can become.\n\n08 — The policy choices\nPolicy matters because markets alone do not automatically produce resilience. Governments may need to build infrastructure, train workers, regulate finance, protect consumers, secure energy, manage debt, negotiate trade rules or coordinate with allies. Bad policy can waste an opportunity; credible policy can turn it into a durable advantage.\n\n09 — The global dimension\nThe topic also affects the balance of power between countries. Control over routes, minerals, money, technology, energy or data can become geopolitical leverage. This is why companies and governments increasingly treat economic systems as strategic assets, not neutral background conditions.\n\n10 — The future\nPoland’s next phase depends on innovation, defense investment and whether it can keep converging as wages rise. The outcome will depend on whether investment, institutions and social trust move in the same direction. When they do, the issue can become a source of resilience and prosperity. When they do not, it remains a source of instability, dependency or missed opportunity."
  },
